<Layout title="What should I do with a large amount of cash? | RIPL Wealth" description="If you've suddenly accumulated a large amount of cash, the first question isn't where to invest it. It's what the money needs to accomplish and when you may need it."><section class="hero"><div class="wrap article"><div class="eyebrow">MONEY QUESTIONS</div><h1>What should I do with a large amount of cash?</h1><p class="small">By RIPL Wealth | Updated October 2026</p><p class="answer"><strong>If you've suddenly accumulated a large amount of cash, the first question isn't where to invest it. It's what the money needs to accomplish and when you may need it.</strong></p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap article"><h2>Give the cash a purpose before choosing an investment</h2><p>Start with the source of the money, any taxes associated with receiving it and the decisions it needs to fund. Separate emergency reserves and known spending from money intended for long-term goals. Those categories may need different investments and different access.</p><h2>Choose a process you can follow</h2><p>Once the amount available for investing is clear, compare your current portfolio, tolerance for market losses and timeline. Decide how you will put the plan into practice and review it. A clear process can be more useful than waiting for a perfect moment to invest.</p><h2>What could change the answer?</h2><ul class="list"><li>How much needs to remain liquid?</li><li>Are taxes or other obligations still outstanding?</li><li>Is there a major purchase, business opportunity or transition ahead?</li><li>How much of this money is truly long-term?</li></ul><h2>Let's make this real.</h2><div class="callout"><p><strong>Hypothetical example. For illustrative purposes only.</strong></p><p>A business owner receives a large distribution but has upcoming taxes and a possible business purchase. Reserving money for those commitments first helps identify how much can genuinely be invested for personal long-term goals.</p></div><h2>Here's what we’d want to know about you.</h2><ul class="list"><li>What do you want this money to accomplish?</li><li>When might you need it?</li><li>What else do you own?</li><li>What are you most worried about getting wrong?</li><li>If we improved this part of your financial life, what would that allow you to do?</li></ul><p class="answer"><strong>Cash can have an important job: buying you time. The goal isn't to rush it into an investment. It's to decide what each part of the money needs to do next.</strong></p><div class="related"><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/money-questions/">Explore Money Questions</a> · <a href="/money-questions/too-much-cash/">Do I have too much cash?</a></div><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start the Conversation </a><div class="sources"><h2>Source and further reading</h2><p><a href="https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/general-resources/publications-research/info-sheets/lump-sum-payouts-questions-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Investor.gov: Making the Most of Your Lump Sum Payment</a></p></div><div class="author-box"><h3>RIPL Wealth</h3><p>Independent wealth planning team helping successful individuals and business owners make better decisions with the wealth they've already built.</p><a href="/about/">About RIPL </a></div></div></section></Layout>
